    "slug": "what-exactly-is-an-antioxidant",
    "originalUrl": "https://fruitypuppy.com/f/what-exactly-is-an-antioxidant",
    "title": "what EXACTLY is an antioxidant?",
    "description": "Hey Ipo… what exactly is an antioxidant, where does it live, how does it work, and how does it get into my skin?” Hi Human. Let’s do this properly. What an an",
    "published": "2026-04-19T03:00:25.937Z",
    "featured": "https://img1.wsimg.com/isteam/ip/fe4bdf6c-22e4-45eb-95e5-8dcff390b5aa/1000034924.jpg",
    "featuredAlt": "what EXACTLY is an antioxidant?",
    "text": "Hey Ipo… what exactly is an antioxidant, where does it live, how does it work, and how does it get into my skin?”\n\nHi Human.\nLet’s do this properly.\n\n\n\nWhat an antioxidant actually is\n\n\n\nAn antioxidant is a specific type of molecule that can give away energy and remain stable afterward.\n\nMore precisely:\n\nIt can donate an electron or hydrogen atom without becoming reactive itself.\n \n\nThat ability comes from its structure—usually phenolic rings or long chains of alternating bonds—which let the molecule absorb and spread energy internally instead of destabilizing.\n\nSo when something reactive tries to cause damage, the antioxidant intercepts it and stops the reaction from continuing.\n\n\n\nWhere antioxidants actually live\n\n\n\nAntioxidants are not loose inside fruit. They are physically contained inside plant cells:\n\nWater compartments (vacuoles) hold vitamin C and polyphenols \n\nCell membranes hold fat-soluble antioxidants like vitamin E and carotenoids \n\nPigment systems hold anthocyanins and flavonoids (the reds, purples, oranges) \n\nThey are locked inside microscopic structures.\n \n\nIf those structures aren’t broken correctly, the antioxidants stay trapped.\n\n\n\nHow they are released from plants\n\nThis is the first place most products fail.\n\nHeat extraction damages fragile compounds.\nPowdering leads to oxidation and loss.\nEssential oil distillation captures scent—but not the antioxidant systems.\n\nFruity Puppy uses freeze-driven rupture:\n\nWater inside the plant expands when frozen \n\nCell walls break from the inside \n\nAntioxidants are released without heat damage \n\nThis preserves the full system:\n\nVitamin C \n\nPolyphenols \n\nEnzymes \n\nSupporting cofactors \n\nHow they are protected and delivered\n\n\n\nOnce released, antioxidants are unstable in open air. They degrade quickly.\n\nSo the problem becomes:\n How do you keep them alive and get them into the skin?\n\nFruity Puppy uses a fat lattice delivery system:\n\nShea, mango, and cocoa butters form a structured crystal network \n\nCoconut oil softens and mobilizes \n\nLecithin helps bind water-based and fat-based components together \n\nThis creates a matrix that:\n\nPhysically holds plant actives in place \n\nProtects them from oxygen and light \n\nReleases them gradually into the skin \n\nYour skin barrier is fat-based, so this structure allows actual transfer instead of surface loss.\n\n\n\nWhy we don’t use essential oils\n\nEssential oils are produced with heat and are made of volatile aromatic compounds.\n\nThey smell strong—but they are missing:\n\nWater-phase antioxidants (like vitamin C) \n\nMost polyphenols \n\nEnzymes and full plant systems \n\nThey are scent fractions, not full biological systems.\n \n\nFruity Puppy uses whole fruit, so you get the complete antioxidant network—not just aroma.\n\n\n\nWhat happens when they reach your skin\n\nWhen delivered properly, antioxidants:\n\nInterrupt damaging reactions at the skin surface \n\nReduce inflammatory signaling \n\nProtect lipids in your skin barrier \n\nSupport collagen stability over time \n\nBut this only works if they:\n\nSurvive extraction \n\nStay stable in the formula \n\nReach the skin interface \n\nMost products fail one of those steps.\nThis system is built to handle all three.\n\n\n\nFinal Ipo translation\n\nAn antioxidant is a molecule that can absorb instability without becoming unstable.\n \n\nPlants store them inside cells.\nMost processes destroy or miss them.\n\nFruity Puppy:\n\nbreaks the cell to access them \n\nprotects them in a structured fat system \n\ndelivers them in a form your skin can actually use \n\nThat’s the whole mechanism."
